**Prefix sums**

First, let's introduce a problem representative of what we call a prefix sum problem. 
Let an array $A$ consisting of length $N \leq 2 \cdot 10^5$ and we are given $Q \leq 2 \cdot 10^5$ queries on it : 
for each query, given $L \leq R$, we have to calculate the sum of the elements between $L$ and $R$ :

Let's try the easiest algorithm, that is, to compute for each interval the sum of the elements in it : this, will, of course, lead to an algorithm of complexity $O(N \cdot Q)$, which is too slow. 

We are then forced to retreat, and consider a simplified version of the problem : 

The first things that come to our mind is $L = R$ for all queries, but that is too easy and don't give much clue for the generalized problem. 

Then, let us remember the title of this problem, prefix sums : maybe we have to consider the prefix, so to fix $L = 1$ for all queries : then, at first sight, the problem looks simple to grasp it, but not so easy : this can be a clue to the main problem. Let's try to go deeper into this simplified problem.  

After thinking for a few minutes, we can realise that the queries are quite limited, that is, there can be many queries that are the same : in a query, there may ask to calculate sum of $[1, 4]$, and ask us $[1, 4]$, then again ask us $[1, 4]$, which induces us to precompute all the sum of intervals $[1, i]$ from $(1 \leq i \leq N)$ in another array, and then print these elements whenever needed. 

Then, now how can we calculate efficiently the long awaited prefix sums ? 

To achieve this, we must first draw the prefix sums, to grasp some spatial sense of the problem. 
Let's consider $p_5$ and $p_6$, respectively the prefix sum of $5$ and the prefix sum of $6$. 
It gives this : 
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/112754140/188275566-dfb15865-845d-48a3-9b35-3aedd9db67b0.png)

Doesn't the two prefix sums resemble ? 

Yes, they are just the same but $p_6$ is $p_5$ added with $a_6$ ! This gives us the following equation : 
$$p_6 = p_5 + a_6$$
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/112754140/188275738-f1f2df82-50d3-4eab-9b6d-a6cc03ed7979.png)

This means that we can calculate $p_6$ quickly by calculating $p_5$. Then let us do the same thing with $p_5$ : we can calculate $p_5$ by calculating $p_4$, $p_4$ by $p_3$, and continuing it, to calculate $p_0$, which is just $0$. 

Here is the following implementation for this simplified problem :

```CPP
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main() {
  // note that the arrays are 1-based indexed. 
  int N; cin >> N;
  int array[N + 1];
  int prefix[N + 1];

  // reading array
  for (int i = 1; i <= N; i ++)
    cin >> array[i];

  // precomputing prefix values
  prefix[0] = 0;
  for (int i = 1; i <= N; i ++)
    prefix[i] = prefix[i - 1] + array[i];

  // answering to the queries
  int Q; cin >> Q;
  for (int i = 1; i <= Q; i ++) {
    int L, R;
    cin >> L >> R;
    // assuming that L = 1
    cout << prefix[R] << '\n';
  }
}

```
Be careful that the prefix values can quickly rise up to $32$-bit values. 

But we aren't finished yet : 

We have to calculate efficiently the sum of elements from index $L$ to $R$, and we will probably need to use the prefix sums; let us draw the interval from $3$ à $7$ for example : 
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/112754140/188277523-0626d63a-9fb9-44e0-8e97-e9364da0052e.png)

Let us consider $p_3$ and $p_7$ , then let us do all the four operations we can do on $p_3$ and $p_7$ : 

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/112754140/188277697-cc2fab46-bacc-44b6-929b-b251f98a817a.png)


Multiplying and dividing one by the other is just nonsense, additionning not really giving much advance, and let us consider substracting $p_7$ by $p_3$ : this gives the sum of interval $[3, 7]$, but slightly different though, interval $[4, 7]$. 

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/112754140/188277752-5777f46e-99a9-4723-acb4-f410e7204ca1.png)

But this means that we are close to our goal : let us change $p_3$ by $p_2$, then it is easy to realise that we got our interval !

The implementation follows : 
```CPP
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main() {
  // note that the arrays are 1-based indexed. 
  int N; cin >> N;
  int array[N + 1];
  int prefix[N + 1];

  // reading array
  for (int i = 1; i <= N; i ++)
    cin >> array[i];

  // precomputing prefix values
  prefix[0] = 0;
  for (int i = 1; i <= N; i ++)
    prefix[i] = prefix[i - 1] + array[i];

  // answering to the queries
  int Q; cin >> Q;
  for (int i = 1; i <= Q; i ++) {
    int L, R;
    cin >> L >> R;
    cout << prefix[R] - prefix[L - 1] << '\n';
  }
}

```

You can notice that it was quite smart to note the arrays $1$-indexed, or else when we consider $L = 0$, then we will have to take $\text{prefix}[0 - 1 = -1]$, which is obviously out of the bounds of the array. Nevertheless, we could have separated the case when $L = 0$, where for this case print $\text{prefix}[R]$, but this is just costing us implementation, and thus disrupting our beautiful and concise implementation.

